{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1744 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2ebfe865ddd1efe7b06b2297edf3dbe262ee444e183c5d62e6e52e864454739f" :edb/db-before-t 1743 :edb/db-after-t 1744 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535056 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:45:59.288Z" 13194139535056 true] [17592186082923 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x$." 13194139535056 false] [17592186082923 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139535056 true] [17592186082923 1145 "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\tan xdx &= \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\frac{\\sin xdx}{\\cos x} \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{0}\\frac{-du}{u} \\\\\n&= - \\int_{1}^{0}\\frac{du}{u} \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{du}{u}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{du}{u} &= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}\\int_{a}^{1}\\frac{du}{u} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}\\ln \\mid u \\mid \\mid _{a}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}\\left[\\ln\\lvert 1\\rvert-\\ln\\lvert a\\rvert\\right] \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}[0 - \\ln \\mid a \\mid ] \\\\\n&= - (- \\infty) \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." 13194139535056 false] [17592186082923 1145 "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & =-(-\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." 13194139535056 true] [17592186083016 1100 "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x-2}\\qquad$  II. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{0} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x+2}\\qquad$  III. $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{3} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x^2-1}$" 13194139535056 true] [17592186083016 1100 "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x-2}\\qquad$ II. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{0} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x+2}\\qquad$ III. $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{3} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x^2-1}$" 13194139535056 false] [17592186083016 1145 "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite. So, it is not an improper integral of the second kind.\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow (-2)^+.\n$$\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits, and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in[2,3].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535056 true] [17592186083016 1145 "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite. So, it is not an improper integral of the second kind.\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow (-2)^+$.\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits, and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in[2,3]$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535056 false] [17592186083059 1145 "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,{\\color{blue}{1}})$:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {1}{x^2-1} \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{1}} ^-\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0, b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to{\\color{blue}1}^-$:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}} = \\lim\\limits_{b \\to {\\color{blue}{1}}^-} \\left(\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{b} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}} \\right)\n$$" 13194139535056 false] [17592186083059 1145 "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,{\\color{blue}{1}})\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) =   \\dfrac {1}{x^2-1} \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{1}} ^-\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0, b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to{\\color{blue}1}^-\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}} = \\lim\\limits_{b \\to {\\color{blue}{1}}^-} \\left( \\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{b} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}}   \\right)\n$$" 13194139535056 true] [17592186083117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328821 13194139535056 true] [17592186083117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328870 13194139535056 true] [17592186083117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328880 13194139535056 true] [17592186083117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328901 13194139535056 true] [17592186083214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328814 13194139535056 true] [17592186083214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328835 13194139535056 true] [17592186083214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328842 13194139535056 true] [17592186083214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328849 13194139535056 true] [17592186083214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328887 13194139535056 true] [17592186083254 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328828 13194139535056 true] [17592186083254 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328856 13194139535056 true] [17592186083254 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328863 13194139535056 true] [17592186083254 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328873 13194139535056 true] [17592186083254 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328894 13194139535056 true] [17592186083745 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535056 true] [17592186083745 1100 "${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}}=$" 13194139535056 true] [17592186083745 1100 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{dt}{\\sqrt{4 - t}}$?" 13194139535056 false] [17592186083745 1145 "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=4-t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}} & ={\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{3}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{2{u}^{1/2}|}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{2\\sqrt{u}|}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[2\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{a}] \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}-0 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328814 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328815 13194139535056 true] [17592186328814 1089 "q-115206" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328814 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535056 true] [17592186328814 1100 "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328814 1113 #uuid "1c5f2100-5d65-54c0-a496-a5ee04c005c6" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328814 1145 "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535056 true] [17592186328815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328816 13194139535056 true] [17592186328815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328817 13194139535056 true] [17592186328815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328818 13194139535056 true] [17592186328815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328819 13194139535056 true] [17592186328815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328820 13194139535056 true] [17592186328815 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535056 true] [17592186328815 1041 "selection" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328815 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328818 13194139535056 true] [17592186328815 1110 #uuid "cbb3c69d-810f-5c22-91f7-a6ea8e75e9c7" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328816 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535056 true] [17592186328816 1111 #uuid "92a68b38-f5e7-5b04-bedd-97ac1661d2f8" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328817 1034 "I only" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535056 true] [17592186328817 1111 #uuid "0958cc90-ff3a-532f-9