atorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328870 1113 #uuid "1dc9e4a0-dba4-54bb-8093-9583c6a7e4ff" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328870 1145 "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)={u}^{-1/5}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{5}{4}{u}^{4/5}|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4/5}-\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {a}^{4/5}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328871 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328872 13194139535056 true] [17592186328871 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535056 true] [17592186328871 1041 "field-1" 13194139535056 true] [17592186328871 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328872 13194139535056 true] [17592186328871